'''Vlex Onopin''' was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] [[scientist]] and [[programmer]] who earned a diploma from the prestigious [[Magrody Institute of Programmable Intelligence]]. After being unwillingly forced into [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] service around [[19 BBY]], the [[engineer]] refined his expertise in the [[kyber crystal]] research field. During that [[time]], Onopin worked in a division of the [[Tarkin Initiative]] known as the [[kyber crystal research team]], which was based on the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]] [[Eadu]]. Onopin and his colleagues [[Project Stardust|worked]] under the leadership of the [[Crystallography|crystallographer]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]] to turn kyber [[crystal]]s into a viable energy source for the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star's]] [[Mk I Superlaser|superlaser]], giving it the capability to destroy entire planets.

In [[1 BBY]], Imperial [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]] found evidence that a member of Onopin's research team had [[Treason|conspired]] with a [[Bodhi Rook|cargo pilot]] to send a message to the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel Alliance]] about the [[Death Star]] project. Krennic brought [[Krennic's guard squadron|death troopers]] to confront Onopin and his colleagues, instructing the traitor to expose himself. Although appreciative of Onopin's talents, the director proceeded to have his [[Death trooper|troopers]] [[Death|execute]] the entire research team despite Erso admitting to the betrayal.

==Biography==
===Forced conscription===
[[File:TarkinInitiative.svg|thumb|left|215px|Vlex Onopin's Imperial service was spent in a division of the Tarkin Initiative (emblem depicted).]]
The [[human]] [[Gender|male]]<ref name="RO" /> Vlex Onopin was a [[scientist]] and [[programmer]] who attended the [[Magrody Institute of Programmable Intelligence]], graduating with a diploma from the prestigious institution<ref name="Geektionary" /> at some point by the end of the [[Republic Era]]. Following the [[Proclamation of the New Order|formation]] of the [[Galactic Empire]] in [[19 BBY]],<ref name="Date" /> he was forcibly conscripted into Imperial service to serve on the [[kyber crystal research team]], a division of the [[Tarkin Initiative]] managed by [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]].<ref name="ROUVG" /> As the team's lead programmer,<ref name="Geektionary" /> Onopin became well-versed in the field of [[kyber crystal]] research.<ref name="ROUVG" /> Identification records of the scientists working for Krennic,<ref name="Dossier">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Rebel Dossier]]''</ref> including Onopin,<ref name="ROUVG" /> were deleted from public databases upon their admittance to Imperial service.<ref name="Dossier" />

Onopin and his colleagues were stationed in the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]] [[Eadu]]'s [[Eadu Energy Conversion Laboratory|Energy Conversion Laboratory]], where they were kept secluded in [[Laboratory|laboratories]]<ref name="ROUVG" /> hidden by the world's [[mountain]]ous terrain.<ref name="SW AB">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know, Updated and Expanded]]''</ref> The programmer and the rest of his team labored on a [[Project Stardust|project]] that would utilize kyber [[crystal]]s as an energy source for the [[Mk I Superlaser|superlaser]] of the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]], a [[Geonosian]]-designed [[battle station]] that would, with the [[superlaser]], possess the capability to destroy entire planets. While working in Eadu's laboratories, Onopin established coded sequences that calculated the<ref name="Geektionary" /> hyperspatial<ref name="ROUVG" /> formulas required for the energy translations of kyber crystals. He spent the majority of his [[time]] in Imperial service fine-tuning these sequencing devices with his coding, for every shot fired by the [[Death Star]]'s superlaser would someday rely on the variables calculated by them.<ref name="Geektionary" />

The kyber crystal research team's [[Standard day|day]]-to-day needs on Eadu were managed by [[Sirro Argonne]], its operations manager.<ref name="ROUVG" /> By [[13 BBY]],<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> work on the superlaser had stalled<ref name="RO NOVEL">[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story (novelization)|''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story'' novelization]]</ref> and left the project [[Standard year|years]] behind schedule,<ref name="Rise">''[[Star Wars: The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''</ref> prompting Krennic to [[Capture of Galen Erso|hunt down]] his former colleague, the [[Crystallography|crystallographer]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]], and force him back into Imperial service.<ref name="Geektionary" /> Onopin and his team proved able to complete the Death Star's superlaser after several [[Standard year|years]] working under Erso, aided by the crystallographer's extraordinary intellect.<ref name="RO NOVEL" />

===Imperial execution===
{{Quote|Gentlemen, one of you betrayed the Empire. One of you has conspired with a pilot to send messages to the Rebellion. And I urge that traitor to step forward.|Director Orson Krennic, confronting Vlex Onopin and his colleagues|audio=KrennicScientistsEadu.flac|<ref name="RO" />}}
[[File:GalenErsoAndFellowScientists-RogueOne3.png|thumb|right|180px|Vlex Onopin (center foreground) and his colleagues learn about Orson Krennic's unplanned visit.]]
In [[1 BBY]],<ref name="Date" /> [[Grand Moff]] [[Wilhuff Tarkin]] informed Krennic that a [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|rebel]] [[Treason|sympathizer]] within Erso's research team had conspired with a [[Bodhi Rook|cargo pilot]] to deliver a message to the Rebel Alliance concerning the Death Star. Enraged, Krennic traveled to Eadu with a [[Krennic's guard squadron|group]] of [[death trooper]]s to investigate,<ref name="RO" /> informing the laboratory's workers, including Onopin, of his [[ST-149|shuttle's]] impending arrival.<ref name="RO 3">[[Rogue One Adaptation 3|''Rogue One Adaptation'' 3]]</ref> Although Krennic's visit worried the programmer, he was annoyed at the director's visit interrupting his work with bureaucratic nonsense. [[Stormtrooper]]s ushered the scientists out of the laboratories<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> to near the entrance of Krennic's [[Delta-class T-3c shuttle|shuttle]].<ref name="RO JUNIOR">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: A Junior Novel]]''</ref> The director then recalled each scientist by name, ordering his death [[trooper]] unit to assemble near the scientists.<ref name="RO NOVEL" />

Krennic announced his knowledge of the conspiracy against the Empire and instructed the traitor to expose himself.<ref name="RO" /> As the director surveyed the team of scientists for signs of guilt, he noticed Onopin opening and closing his [[mouth]] as if begging for the turncoat to reveal himself.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> When Krennic received no response in the moments afterward, he concluded that the scientists had conspired in a group effort. As Krennic's death troopers readied themselves to [[Death|execute]] the scientists, Erso intervened and confessed to being the traitor in an attempt to save Onopin and his colleagues. However, as soon as Erso stepped toward the director, his scientists were shot and killed by the death troopers per Krennic's orders.<ref name="RO" />

Onopin's work contributed to the completion of the Death Star project,<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> and his coding sequences were used by the [[space station]] during its subsequent deployments. However, the Death Star was ultimately [[Battle of Yavin|destroyed]] not long after Onopin's death when the Rebel Alliance targeted a faulty [[thermal exhaust port]]<ref name="Geektionary" /> that Erso had deliberately planted inside.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> In [[35 ABY]],<ref name="RiseDate">{{RiseAndFallDate}}</ref> the [[historian]] [[Beaumont Kin]] negatively compared the team that Onopin was part of to Erso in<ref name="Rise" /> his<ref name="TROSVD">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref> [[book]] ''[[The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''.<ref name="Rise" />

==Personality and traits==
{{Quote|In Erso's absence Krennic was forced to try and persevere with his other captive scientists, none of whom possessed the raw intellect of the man he had lost.|Beaumont Kin, ''The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire''|<ref name="Rise" />}}
When Onopin was forced into Imperial service, his passion for programming diminished despite the excellent sequences he still produced.<ref name="ROUVG" /> The time he spent in the kyber crystal research team's laboratories on Eadu left him frail and unused to the outdoors by the time of his death.<ref name="RO JUNIOR" /> Onopin disliked interruptions to his work, regarding Krennic's initial arrival on Eadu as bureaucratic nonsense. Although Krennic reflected on how much he appreciated the programmer, the director ordered his execution alongside his colleagues' regardless. When confronted by Krennic's death troopers, Onopin was terrified at the prospect of death and looked from side to side in an effort to convince the traitor to reveal himself.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> In his book, Kin expressed his opinion that neither Onopin or any of Krennic's other captive scientists possessed Erso's "raw intellect."<ref name="Rise" /> The programmer had [[Color|pale]] skin, brown [[eye]]s, a gray beard, and brown [[hair]] that was graying by the time of his death.<ref name="ROUVG" />

==Skills and abilities==
A gifted programmer, Onopin was extremely talented at forming new coding sequences. During his Imperial service, he became exceptionally skilled in the field of kyber crystal research.<ref name="Geektionary" />

==Equipment==
[[File:EaduScientists.png|thumb|left|250px|Onopin (left) wore a blue-and-white engineering jumpsuit while part of the kyber crystal research team.]]
While on Eadu, Onopin wore a long-sleeved, blue-and-white engineering [[Clothing|jumpsuit]].<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> His jumpsuit's [[antiseptic coveralls]] sported the dark-gray [[Imperial crest|insignia]] of the Empire on the left sleeve and a chest pocket that held two [[code cylinder]]s programmed with access to restricted areas.<ref name="ROUVG" /> The fractured kyber crystal&ndash;inspired<ref name="SW AB" /> logo of the Tarkin Initiative was present on the chest of Onopin's coveralls.<ref name="ROUVG" />

The programmer's uniform included shoes that were grounded against potential static charges present within laboratories. He also donned a tunic that would change colors when in the presence of harmful [[radiation]]. Onopin's work on Eadu centered around using various [[computer]]s to code sequences for use in the Death Star.<ref name="ROUVG" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|For the Visual Guide, gave a bunch of these scientists names that were basically phonemes made up of Los Alamos scientists' names chopped up, tossed into a blender and stuck together again.|Pablo Hidalgo|<ref name="Twitter">{{Twitter|username=pabl0hidalgo|url=status/1727942382644256970|name=[[Pablo Hidalgo]]|date=2023-11-24|quote=For the Visual Guide, gave a bunch of these scientists names that were basically phonemes made up of Los Alamos scientists' names chopped up, tossed into a blender and stuck together again.|archivefile=File:PabloLosAlamosTweet.png}}</ref>}}
[[File:EaduScientistsConcept.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Jon McCoy's concept artwork of the Eadu scientists' deaths for ''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story''.]]
Vlex Onopin first appeared in the [[Star Wars Anthology Series|Anthology]] film, ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]'',<ref name="RO" /> as well as in its [[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story (novelization)|adult]]<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> and [[Star Wars: Rogue One: A Junior Novel|junior novelizations]],<ref name="RO JUNIOR" /> which released the same day as the film on [[December 16]], [[2016]].<ref name="SW.com">{{SW|url=news/rebel-reading-rogue-one-books|text=Rebel Reading: A Guide to ''Rogue One'' Books|author=[[James Floyd|Floyd, James]]|date=2016-12-14}}</ref> The scientist was portrayed by the actor [[Weston Gavin]].<ref name="RO" /> He was first pictured in a [https://web.archive.org/web/20240206211410/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sC9abcLLQpI trailer] for the film released on the official ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[Wikipedia:YouTube|YouTube]] channel on [[October 13]] of the same year.<ref name="Trailer">{{SWYouTube|sC9abcLLQpI|Rogue One: A Star Wars Story Trailer #2 (Official)|sw_url=video/rogue-one-a-star-wars-story-trailer-2}}</ref> Although the film's credits simply refer to him as "Engineer,"<ref name="RO" /> For ''Rogue One'', concept artwork of the scientists' death scene was created by artist [[Jon McCoy]].<ref name="Artbook">''[[The Art of Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref>

Onopin's name was revealed in both ''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: The Ultimate Visual Guide]]'', an accompanying reference book written by [[Pablo Hidalgo]],<ref name="ROUVG" /> and in ''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Secret Mission]]'', a [[Dorling Kindersley|DK Readers]] title authored by [[Jason Fry]],<ref name="ROSM">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Secret Mission]]''</ref> which were both released alongside the film on December 16.<ref name="SW.com" /> Hidalgo created several of the scientists' names by combining elements from the names of the real-life scientists associated with the [[Wikipedia:Los Alamos National Laboratory|Los Alamos National Laboratory]].<ref name="Twitter" /> Onopin was pictured in a card made as a promo for the ''[[Secrets of Power]]'' set of the [[Fantasy Flight Games]] [[Star Wars: Unlimited|''Star Wars'': Unlimited]] card game<ref name="SWU">{{SWU|set=Secrets of Power|cardname=Galen Erso|subtitle=You'll Never Win|type=PQ Participation|link=4229452447}}</ref> that was released on [[November 7]], [[2025]].<ref name="SSU">{{UnlimitedWeb|url=products/set-6-secrets-of-power|text=Set 6 Secrets of Power}}</ref> The card's artwork was drawn by [[Axel Hutt]] in [[2024]].<ref name="Axel">{{Bluesky|smirt.bsky.social|post/3mdlzt3vl522j|[[Axel Hutt]]|date=2026-01-29|quote="Galen Erso" for Star Wars Unlimited. I drew that in 2024.
Also, abolish ICE.|archiveurl=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/CXVcW}}</ref>

